---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
import Capture from "../components/site/Capture.astro";
---
<Site
  title="Writ privacy"
  description="Writ keeps your text on your disk. No telemetry, no account. The one feature that can send text off the machine is off by default."
  path="/privacy/"
>
  <main class="page" id="top">
  <section class="band doc">
    <div class="pr-wrap">
      <header class="pr-head">
        <span class="doc-eyebrow">privacy · reviewed 2026-08-24</span>
        <h1 class="doc-h1">Where your text lives, and what leaves the machine.</h1>
      </header>
      <Capture name="still-settings" alt="Writ settings" class="shot-win" sizes="(max-width: 880px) 100vw, 720px" />

      <div class="doc-body pr-body">
        <p>
          Writ stores your text in <code>~/.writ</code>. Each buffer's text is a plain file under
          <code>buffers/</code>. Next to it, <code>writ.db</code> holds the buffer metadata and a copy of the text that
          full-text search reads; a file opened above 5&nbsp;MB is left out of that index. Writ has no server side and
          no account.
          <code>Settings &rarr; Storage</code> shows the full path to <code>writ.db</code>, inside that directory.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same directory holds <code>config.toml</code> and <code>logs/</code>, which records what the app did and
          the paths of files and folders you opened, not the text inside them. Logs rotate daily and are not deleted on
          a schedule. Anything you pipe into the <code>writ</code> CLI is also written to <code>piped/</code> and kept there.
        </p>

        <h2>What the app does on the network</h2>
        <p>
          With rewriting off, which is the default, the app makes one kind of outbound request: it fetches a static
          release manifest from GitHub and compares your platform and version on your machine. That happens on its own
          shortly after launch, no more than once a day, and whenever it is asked for in
          <code>Settings &rarr; Updates</code>. The request carries nothing about you beyond what any HTTP request
          carries. Turn the automatic one off with <code>Check for updates automatically</code>. Installing an update
          downloads the release from GitHub and checks it against a signing key built into the app.
        </p>
        <p>
          Spell check runs against a dictionary bundled in the app and makes no requests.
        </p>
        <p>
          The preview pane runs under a fixed content-security-policy (<code>default-src 'none'</code>) in an isolated origin.
          Rendered HTML makes no network calls.
        </p>
        <p>
          Writ sends no crash reports, usage data, or telemetry.
        </p>

        <h2>Rewriting text</h2>
        <p>
          Writ can rewrite a selection through an OpenAI-compatible endpoint. This is the only feature that can send what
          you write off your machine. It is off by default; turn it on in <code>Settings &rarr; Rewrite</code>. The
          default endpoint is a local Ollama server at <code>http://localhost:11434/v1</code>, which keeps the request
          on your machine.
        </p>
        <p>
          Point it at a hosted provider and Writ asks once, naming the host, before it sends that host anything: the
          text you rewrite, or the check that the endpoint is reachable. The answer is recorded for that host alone, so
          agreeing to one provider does not cover another or a hand-edited URL. The request body carries the text being
          rewritten, the instruction for the action you picked, and the model id. With nothing selected, Writ asks
          before sending the whole buffer instead.
        </p>
        <p>
          While the feature is on, Writ checks whether the endpoint is reachable by requesting its model list, from
          Settings and from the Rewrite menu in the status bar. That request carries your API key and no document
          text, and it is not sent to a hosted endpoint you have not agreed to.
        </p>
        <p>
          On macOS and Windows the API key is stored in the system keychain, under the service <code>com.writ.ai</code>.
          On other platforms it is held in memory for the session and is gone on restart. It is never written to
          <code>config.toml</code>.
        </p>

        <h2>What this site does</h2>
        <p>
          This site is a static export served from GitHub Pages. It self-hosts its fonts.
          It counts page views with a self-hosted Umami instance at stats.ibrahemid.com, which stores
          no IP addresses and shares nothing with third parties. It sets no cookies and does not embed
          third-party widgets.
        </p>

        <h2>Backup and deletion</h2>
        <p>
          Copy <code>~/.writ</code> to back up; <code>writ.db</code> on its own leaves the buffer text behind.
        </p>
        <p>
          Delete <code>~/.writ</code> to return the app to a fresh install. Files you saved elsewhere on disk are
          untouched. An API key is not in that directory: clear it from <code>Settings &rarr; Rewrite</code>, or from
          your keychain.
        </p>

        <h2>Source of truth</h2>
        <p>
          Everything above is in the repository:
          <a href="https://github.com/ibrahemid/writ" rel="noopener" target="_blank">github.com/ibrahemid/writ</a>.
          If you find a network call this page does not describe, file an issue and it will be treated as a bug.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  </main>

  <style>
    .pr-wrap { max-width: var(--measure); display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1rem; padding-bottom: 6rem; }
    .pr-head { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.5rem; }
  </style>
</Site>
